      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "When it comes to debugging, from a programmer's point of view, it does matter. To the computer, there is a big difference between \" \" and \"%20\", even if most applications can auto convert \"%20\" to \" \" and vice versa. I chose to pose the question to help the developers in their debugging efforts. The more information you give a developer about how to reproduce a problem, the easier it is to find and fix and thus, the less time it takes to fix the bug.\n\nIt's better for a ticket to say \"X is not working under conditions X Y and Z, but it does work for A B and C\" than if it said, \"X is not working\". Imagine if this report was titled \"VideoSyncPlayer can't handle URLs\". Would that be accurate? Not really, since it works with most URLs just fine.\n\nTo be quite honest, I wasn't being picky when I asked my question. Now that the \"more information needed\" has been brought up, let's break things down based off the title and the information provided in the ticket.\n\n\"VideoSyncPlayer can't handle URLs with ] + space in them\"\nFrom the title, we can tell that the VideoSyncPlayer script does not work with a literal right bracket, followed by a space. The description appears to support that, along with the fact that having a right bracket and no space works. Here are a few questions that, if answered, would provide a world of information without me (as a developer) having to try myself.\n\n1. Have you tried a left bracket then a space?\n2. Was a \" \" the only thing that was tried?\n3. Have you tried replacing the space with %20?\n4. Does quoting the URL change the effect?\n5. If spaces work without being preceded by a right bracket, does it break with other characters besides a right bracket?\n\nEach of those questions helps to clarify what the actual problem is. I did not see any of them answered in the OP. Saying that the answers to them don't matter isn't factually accurate since they do aid in the development process.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
